    {
      "kind": "statement",
      "eyebrow": "01 · The thesis",
      "title": "The technology is not the story. The cost line is.",
      "body": "This sector sells connectivity, attention and content. AI lowers the cost of producing content and raises the value of distribution and first-party data. Networks become software-defined and capex-heavy, while the interface to the customer moves from apps to assistants that summarise, recommend and block advertising."
    },
    {
      "kind": "points",
      "eyebrow": "02 · Sector economics",
      "title": "What this sector actually sells",
      "body": "Telecom is a capital-intensive utility with ARPU and churn as the key variables. Media and platforms earn on advertising yield and subscriptions, with content cost as the swing factor.",
      "bullets": [
        "Mobile and broadband ARPU grows slowly; churn and acquisition cost decide profitability.",
        "Network capex is lumpy and regulated returns depend on utilisation.",
        "Advertising earns on impressions, targeting and measurement.",
        "Content businesses carry high fixed costs and hit-driven revenue.",
        "Bundling reduces churn but compresses margin per service."
      ]
    },
    {
